>
  <p>Rep ranges decide what a set trains. Strength coach Jordan Hoppel breaks them down by goal.</p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Goal</th><th>Reps</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Strength</td><td>3 to 5</td><td>Don't go to failure often; complete the programmed reps.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Muscle (hypertrophy)</td><td>6 to 12 typically</td><td>Get close to failure; can range from 5 to 30.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Endurance</td><td>30+</td><td>More aerobic than anaerobic.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Strength: the three to five method</h2>
  <p>3 to 5 exercises, 3 to 5 sets, 3 to 5 reps, 3 to 5 days a week. Strength work is task-driven: a certain weight for a certain number of reps.</p>

  <h2>Muscle size: proximity to failure</h2>
  <p>Hypertrophy is typically 6 to 12 reps, but it's very individual. Proximity to failure is easier to measure around 6 to 8 reps than at 10 to 12. You can go as low as 5 or as high as 30 reps as long as proximity to failure is the same.</p>

  <JordanQuote>People think they need higher reps than they do because the pump and blood flow feel like it's working more, but mechanical tension is the main driver.</JordanQuote>

  <Media type="image" alt="Diagram of rep ranges for strength, muscle, and endurance" caption="Rep ranges by goal." brief="Graphic of the rep-range table." />

  <h2>Endurance</h2>
  <p>30 reps and over, more aerobic than anaerobic.</p>

  <h2>Strength vs. size percentages</h2>
  <p>The percentages are mostly the same for both goals. The difference is effort: strength work doesn't chase failure, hypertrophy does. For how percentages work, see <a href="/one-rep-max-percentages-explained/">one-rep max percentages explained</a>.</p>
</GuideLayout>
